WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:05.490
Next we'll talk about the difference between parent and instance materials

2
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.169
and how to add a few more features to those functionalities as well.

3
00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:12.609
So we'll review parent materials and instance materials to begin with.

4
00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:16.129
We'll talk about creating some switch parameters for instance in so you can

5
00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:20.210
really dial in the result of what you want a specific instance to look like.

6
00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.929
And we'll talk about creating a master material, a UV control for instance in.

7
00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:30.000
So let's just review the parent materials and the instance in concept.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.399
So when to use it and what and why is it useful.

9
00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:38.799
So you'll see that we have parent material on the right hand side here.

10
00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:45.950
We've got color value, in this example we've decided to create a metallic value

11
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.829
and also a roughness value and we've plugged those into the respective inputs.

12
00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:54.990
Now you'll see that these are all parameters, you'll see in the

13
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.549
slight italicized text below it says param, param, and param,

14
00:00:59.549 --> 00:01:04.109
and that essentially means it's a parameter that we can use to change

15
00:01:04.109 --> 00:01:06.750
the result outside of the parent material.

16
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.180
So on the left hand side here you'll see an instance version of this material

17
00:01:11.819 --> 00:01:15.819
and on the the top properties you'll see that we've got the albedo ticked,

18
00:01:15.819 --> 00:01:18.620
we've got the normal ticked, and we've got the roughness ticked.

19
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:24.140
And we can dial in these values based on the instanced version of that.

20
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.180
So we've created one material here that has parameters that we can change

21
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.859
and on the instance we can make subtle tweaks and shifts.

22
00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:37.019
Sometimes we might want to make large shifts and tweaks,

23
00:01:37.019 --> 00:01:41.340
but essentially you don't have to create a material from scratch,

24
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.700
you can just change the values that you wanted to change

25
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.450
based on the parameter of the master parent material.

26
00:01:49.489 --> 00:01:53.409
So we can think about adding a little bit more functionality

27
00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:57.170
to these materials. The previous example was a little bit more of a basic

28
00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.370
that you'd expect to see in any given unreal project.

29
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.849
Now let's think about how we might switch based on some of these parameters.

30
00:02:04.849 --> 00:02:08.370
So we can import a desired texture, whatever you'd like,

31
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.210
and we'll prompt you at the end of this section to create your own.

32
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.689
So if we just go through the notes to begin with,

33
00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:18.129
and then you can pause the video and try and recreate this yourself.

34
00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:21.090
So you import some kind of desired material,

35
00:02:21.729 --> 00:02:24.689
we'll right click and search for a switch parameter.

36
00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:28.530
So if you just right click in the material graph, type in switch parameter,

37
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.810
you'll have a static switch parameter that will appear there,

38
00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.490
and then you just want to select that.

39
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.210
And then we can create several constraints and plug them into the false node.

40
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.569
So if you want to create a const,

41
00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.330
you can hold down one on your keyboard and left mouse click,

42
00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.849
and that will create a const one.

43
00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:51.250
Alternatively, you can just find the constants in the right click menu.

44
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.159
Scalar parameters, in a similar way,

45
00:02:54.159 --> 00:02:58.400
scalar parameters are constants nodes that have been converted to a parameter.

46
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.280
So once we've created that const,

47
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.719
so we maybe held down number one on our keyboard,

48
00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:05.840
we left clicked,

49
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.879
you can actually right click on that new node that you created

50
00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:11.039
and convert it to a parameter,

51
00:03:11.039 --> 00:03:13.599
and therefore you'll then be able to give it a name.

52
00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:16.159
By default, it'll just be a numerical value,

53
00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:17.919
but if you want it to be a parameter,

54
00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:20.479
you can right click and convert that to a parameter.

55
00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:24.719
So we want to convert all the texture sample nodes to parameters,

56
00:03:24.719 --> 00:03:26.400
including any constant parameters,

57
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.199
they will now become scalar parameters for you to assign names to

58
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:36.319
that we'll be able to communicate with in an instance version of this material.

59
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.050
If we want to think about creating a master material with UV control,

60
00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.449
let's maybe add a UV coordinate.

61
00:03:44.449 --> 00:03:47.009
So just right click UV coordinate,

62
00:03:47.009 --> 00:03:49.090
and this is a text coordinate in this example,

63
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.129
we want to create two multiply expressions,

64
00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:54.129
so we've got one multiply here,

65
00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.409
one multiply there,

66
00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:58.930
you can actually hold down M for multiply on the keyboard,

67
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.009
and to create a multiplier there,

68
00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:03.169
we can create an append expression,

69
00:04:03.169 --> 00:04:05.250
we can create three scalar parameters,

70
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.810
again just holding down one on the keyboard for each one

71
00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.250
and convert them to a parameter,

72
00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:13.009
and then connect them to each texture UV desired.

73
00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:16.370
Just a note on this last section here,

74
00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.290
you'll see the comment box there as well,

75
00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:23.250
but the last multiply acts as a bridge and can be an add instead,

76
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.970
so if you'd like to create an add instead of a multiply,

77
00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:26.930
you can do that,

78
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.209
and it will simply shift the UVs whilst multiply shifts and increases the scale.

79
00:04:32.209 --> 00:04:33.170
So keep in mind,

80
00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:37.329
and it depends on the design effects of what you would like your material to look like.

81
00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.209
To create the instance version of the material,

82
00:04:40.209 --> 00:04:43.490
so we've got this new material in our content browser,

83
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.720
you'll see it wherever you created it in the folder,

84
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.120
you can actually right click on this master or parent material,

85
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.439
and create a material instance based on that parent material.

86
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.699
When you do, a new material will be created,

87
00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:03.500
but it'll be a material instance instead of an actual just master material,

88
00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.660
and we can load up this instance,

89
00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.540
and you'll see some kind of box like this,

90
00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:13.420
it'll have different parameters based on what you had created in the previous example,

91
00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:18.220
but it'll allow you to tick certain parameters on and off of the properties

92
00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.819
that you want to edit based on the material that you're trying to create.

93
00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:26.930
So instances are used to prevent constant recompiling of shaders,

94
00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.209
because we've got this parent or master material,

95
00:05:30.209 --> 00:05:32.769
that's what's doing all your shader compilation,

96
00:05:32.769 --> 00:05:37.009
it knows what to expect from the PBR system that you're trying to create,

97
00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:40.930
and therefore every instance is just based on that parent shader.

98
00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.730
So Unreal knows to calculate certain parameters,

99
00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.689
and then it's just dialing in the specific result that you want.

100
00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:49.410
So we can make the instance from the master material,

101
00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.850
we can open the instance, click the nose to activate them,

102
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.850
swap out any textures you would like,

103
00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:58.290
and it gives you a lot more freedom and flexibility,

104
00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.610
and it's also just less performant,

105
00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:04.610
well it's more performant but less heavy on performance to take this approach.

106
00:06:05.329 --> 00:06:08.290
So maybe it's worth jumping in now into your editor,

107
00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:12.689
maybe think about creating a new parent material with some switch parameters,

108
00:06:12.689 --> 00:06:16.370
you could create the UV parameter control as we just went over in the video,

109
00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.529
and maybe just test them out, test that workflow,

110
00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:24.209
get familiar with it, it's a concept that you'll use in all your Unreal projects,

111
00:06:24.209 --> 00:06:26.850
it's just a very streamlined way of working,

112
00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.089
it allows a lot of flexibility,

113
00:06:29.089 --> 00:06:33.569
and it's just a useful pipeline to know and understand moving forward.
